module std.prelude

// Rendering a value as text, for code that does not know the value's type.
//
// **The gap this closes.** `print` and `"${…}"` pick a formatter per *concrete* type, so
// a value whose type is a **type parameter** could not be rendered at all: a `t` has no
// representation, and there is nothing to pick. The prelude's own `expect` hit this the
// day it was written — the natural draft reports what it got, `panic("expected ${value}")`,
// and none of it was expressible. With `Show`, a bounded generic can describe its own
// payload:
//
//   let dump<t> where t: Show = (v: t) -> string => "got ${v}"
//
// **A bound is what makes it work, and interpolation reads it.** Inside such a function
// `"${v}"` is rewritten to `v.show()` before anything downstream sees it, so the whole
// feature is the bound dispatch that already existed plus this trait. Without a bound the
// diagnostic says so and names this trait.
//
// **The trait is recognized by its method, not by its name.** The rewrite looks for an
// in-scope bound whose trait declares `show`, so a program may define its own and the
// compiler is none the wiser — the same rule arithmetic operator overloading follows. The
// name `Show` is what the *message* suggests, because this is the one the prelude ships.
pub trait Show {
  show: (Self) -> string
}

// The impls for the printable scalars, and they are **ordinary Lyra**: `"${self}"` on a
// concrete primitive is exactly the formatter `print` already picks, so nothing here is a
// builtin. That is the same division of labour `parse_i64` and `trim` follow — anything
// expressible in the language belongs in the prelude, where it is readable, testable and
// replaceable, and the builtin registry keeps only what is genuinely primitive.
//
// `string`'s impl returns the value rather than interpolating it: `"${s}"` would copy the
// bytes into a fresh box for no reason, and `show` is the one place that cost would be
// paid on every rendering of every string.
impl Show for string { show = (self) => self }

impl Show for bool { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for rune { show = (self) => "${self}" }

impl Show for i8 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for i16 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for i32 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for i64 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for i128 { show = (self) => "${self}" }

impl Show for u8 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for u16 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for u32 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for u64 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for u128 { show = (self) => "${self}" }

impl Show for f16 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for f32 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
impl Show for f64 { show = (self) => "${self}" }
